WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:54.787
[Western music]

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.040
[Peter] Good morning guys.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.612
Here the southeastern corner of Arizona.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.689
Outside of the town of Morenci
off there in the distance.

00:01:07.164 --> 00:01:11.904
Now Morenci is one of the last few
true company towns in America.

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:14.178
Meaning the company
owns all of the real estate.

00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:18.600
Everyone works for the company
either directly or indirectly.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.334
And it's just far removed from the state.

00:01:22.777 --> 00:01:24.227
From the rest of the country.

00:01:24.838 --> 00:01:26.463
And is the biggest

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.820
copper operation in North America.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.510
So today we're going to get out there
on the streets of Morenci.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.659
Talk to the locals and try to get
a better understanding of what it's like

00:01:38.129 --> 00:01:40.603
to live in a modern day company town.

00:01:41.168 --> 00:01:42.168
Let's do this.

00:01:42.169 --> 00:01:43.169
[country music]

00:02:05.703 --> 00:02:07.712
Here we are guys, Morenci.

00:02:09.136 --> 00:02:10.357
Morenci Motel.

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:12.297
We have the grocery store.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Health center.

00:02:14.598 --> 00:02:17.078
And then mine right in the distance there.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:21.134
So roughly 2000 people
living here currently.

00:02:21.521 --> 00:02:24.172
And the mine, from my research is thriving.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.790
Copper prices are up.

00:02:26.487 --> 00:02:28.548
A lot is getting extracted out of here.

00:02:28.801 --> 00:02:30.501
We're going to get up there today.

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:33.626
I think there's actually
a sweet place to see into the pit.

00:02:33.626 --> 00:02:34.741
I think there is.

00:02:35.821 --> 00:02:37.880
You know, those are all company homes.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.650
Nobody owns up here. Everybody is renting.

00:02:42.326 --> 00:02:46.152
To get somebody that works here in the mine
on camera at a higher level,

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:48.159
impossible from my understanding.

00:02:48.483 --> 00:02:50.181
And so I had a guy set up.

00:02:50.468 --> 00:02:53.237
He was so excited
to show me around Morenci,

00:02:53.847 --> 00:02:58.405
get us in one of the homes here, talk at
a deeper level about company town living.

00:02:58.679 --> 00:03:00.315
And his superior said,

00:03:00.662 --> 00:03:02.040
"Nope, can't do that."

00:03:02.041 --> 00:03:05.585
So that's why it's me solo mission.

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:09.085
Well, we'll get a good understanding
today though guys. I feel it here.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.656
-How are you doing?
-Good. How are you?

00:03:11.656 --> 00:03:13.848
[Peter] Good. You've lived here 13 years?

00:03:13.848 --> 00:03:15.126
-Yes.
-How is it?

00:03:15.126 --> 00:03:16.411
It's pretty good.

00:03:16.411 --> 00:03:18.011
It was kind of a shock at first.

00:03:18.604 --> 00:03:21.710
We moved here from Show Low
up in the forest, you know,

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.264
but there's a lot of good people here.

00:03:23.264 --> 00:03:24.785
We've made a lot of friends.

00:03:24.785 --> 00:03:28.102
-What's the company town feel if
you've lived outside of the company town?

00:03:28.102 --> 00:03:30.327
Now you live in it. What's that like?

00:03:30.327 --> 00:03:31.651
It's weird. It's different.

00:03:32.127 --> 00:03:33.127
-How so?

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:36.563
I feel like they're more privileged here

00:03:36.564 --> 00:03:38.545
just because you work for the company,
you know?

00:03:38.545 --> 00:03:41.466
Okay. So no unemployment in town, right?

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:43.395
Not really. No.

00:03:43.395 --> 00:03:46.749
It's good though. The company
really takes good care of everybody.

00:03:47.359 --> 00:03:49.298
-Pays pretty good here?
-Mm-hmm. Yeah.

00:03:49.663 --> 00:03:52.330
When we first moved here,
it was just an empty Dairy Queen building

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.036
and the swimming pool.

00:03:54.502 --> 00:03:57.381
And they built this fitness center here.

00:03:57.382 --> 00:04:01.085
It was kind of crazy because
it doesn't even feel like it belongs here.

00:04:01.085 --> 00:04:03.209
It's so huge. It's super nice.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:04.500
-Health care?
-Yes.

00:04:04.501 --> 00:04:06.634
-Full family. Dental?

00:04:06.952 --> 00:04:09.679
Dental, vision, yeah. All of it.

00:04:09.679 --> 00:04:10.679
-Everything?

00:04:10.882 --> 00:04:13.303
Yeah. I mean, he takes a portion
out of the check, but…

00:04:13.303 --> 00:04:17.394
-What's your rent?
-Our rent right now is three…

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:20.410
-It's like $360 or something like that.
-$360?

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:21.411
Something like that.

00:04:21.411 --> 00:04:24.183
-For a house? For one of these places?
-Yeah. Three bedroom.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.377
Ours is just right down there.
It's a three-bedroom.

00:04:26.377 --> 00:04:27.584
I mean, they're old, but…

00:04:27.584 --> 00:04:30.370
-You're paying like 1986 prices?
-Mm-hmm.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.550
Very blessed here, I feel like.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.301
-Thank you, guys.
-It's nice meeting you.

00:04:34.301 --> 00:04:36.220
-You too.
-Have a good day.

00:04:37.778 --> 00:04:39.578
So I was in Bagdad, guys.

00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:41.724
Bagdad's another one of
these company towns.

00:04:41.724 --> 00:04:45.374
And then people told me, "Hey,
you got to check out Morenci. It's bigger."

00:04:45.822 --> 00:04:48.030
It's a bigger mine. It's a bigger town.

00:04:48.435 --> 00:04:52.480
And I've just taken a fascination
with the concept of it,

00:04:52.486 --> 00:04:55.126
because it's an old concept.

00:04:55.127 --> 00:04:58.133
It's been around
in the United States forever.

00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:01.581
And at one time,
there were thousands of company towns.

00:05:02.141 --> 00:05:04.780
And in those times, it wasn't like this.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.809
I mean, the air quality,
just the way the mines were run,

00:05:08.108 --> 00:05:10.060
the way the workers were treated.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.536
Different universe compared to now,

00:05:14.157 --> 00:05:16.288
where they're setting up
nice gyms and whatnot.

00:05:17.017 --> 00:05:19.396
Look at that baseball field there.

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:22.940
And then, like the lady said,
this was a Dairy Queen

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.025
and they've gone all in in it looks like.

00:05:25.025 --> 00:05:26.461
Morenci Club,

00:05:27.275 --> 00:05:30.798
library, movie theater, Starbucks,

00:05:31.198 --> 00:05:32.324
and the gym.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.337
Community center.

00:05:37.064 --> 00:05:38.709
So first impressions, guys.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:40.373
Morenci, very quiet.

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:43.466
Now it is 1030 on a Saturday.

00:05:44.323 --> 00:05:45.791
I didn't know what day
would be best.

00:05:45.791 --> 00:05:49.834
I thought Monday through Friday,
maybe everyone's working in the mines.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.600
Weekends, they might have more time,
but then

00:05:52.892 --> 00:05:55.473
it might be one of those
the mines never stop.

00:05:55.473 --> 00:05:57.918
So everyone's on a shift.
So it really doesn't matter.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:03.677
[Peter] Community center
for the whole town.

00:06:03.677 --> 00:06:05.031
For the whole town of Morenci.

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:06.340
So people working in the mine?

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:08.962
Working in the mine
or non-Freeport employees.

00:06:08.963 --> 00:06:10.663
Anybody can use our facility.

00:06:10.663 --> 00:06:11.879
Big pool setup.

00:06:11.879 --> 00:06:14.731
Yeah, we've got an indoor pool
and an outdoor pool.

00:06:15.111 --> 00:06:16.210
Ah, that's great.

00:06:16.651 --> 00:06:18.450
-Can I walk in? Is that cool?
-Sure.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.027
-What's your name?
-My name is Sandra.

00:06:20.027 --> 00:06:21.972
-Sandra. Peter.
-Nice to meet you, Peter.

00:06:21.972 --> 00:06:23.119
How long have you been here?

00:06:23.119 --> 00:06:26.598
I've been with the company 21 years,
but in this facility 10.

00:06:27.171 --> 00:06:28.344
-How's it been?

00:06:28.344 --> 00:06:30.251
Fabulous. I love it.

00:06:31.517 --> 00:06:34.852
Okay, so they have
a volleyball set up here.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:38.904
So I've been to West Virginia,
Kentucky.

00:06:38.904 --> 00:06:42.035
I think you guys have probably seen
some of those videos. Some of you have.

00:06:42.035 --> 00:06:45.418
The old mining towns.
And when that mine runs out,

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.820
well, everyone leaves and it's just decay.

00:06:48.041 --> 00:06:49.581
And this is the opposite, right?

00:06:49.581 --> 00:06:52.254
This is the mine
that is thriving currently.

00:06:52.662 --> 00:06:55.057
And from what I read, there's decades

00:06:55.668 --> 00:06:58.686
of copper to be had in that mine.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:00.440
[weights clank]

00:07:08.088 --> 00:07:10.048
[weights bang and clank]

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:12.017
-So you live out here?
-Yeah.

00:07:12.017 --> 00:07:13.167
-Working the mine?
-Yeah.

00:07:13.167 --> 00:07:17.120
How is it? It's cool. I like it. Day off,
you come here, you do some exercise.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:19.490
Where are you from originally?
I'm from Ghana.

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:20.490
-No way!
-Yeah.

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:22.230
How'd you find this place?

00:07:22.651 --> 00:07:24.496
Okay, so I moved into Nevada.

00:07:24.496 --> 00:07:26.077
Then I found this one online.

00:07:26.615 --> 00:07:27.960
-You found it online?
-Yeah.

00:07:28.453 --> 00:07:30.555
How is it living in company town?

00:07:31.828 --> 00:07:33.204
It's a small town, chill.

00:07:33.204 --> 00:07:36.311
Like, low violence.
And I like all the peace.

00:07:36.311 --> 00:07:38.975
-Low violence. Like, no crime here?

00:07:38.975 --> 00:07:40.532
No crime, so I like it chill.

00:07:40.539 --> 00:07:42.989
Come to work out,
goes to the gym, then go to sleep.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.050
-Good pay?
-Yeah. I like it, yeah.

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:50.139
-Company picnic, huh?
-You guys have a company picnic?

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:52.112
-Yeah, Freeport does.
-Freeport does, yeah.

00:07:52.112 --> 00:07:54.028
So there are a lot of events going on?

00:07:54.028 --> 00:07:55.805
Yeah, this whole parking lot is full.

00:07:56.107 --> 00:07:58.252
And the gym and everything, there's just…

00:07:58.252 --> 00:08:02.082
Not just from here, they come from Safford,
they come from Lordsburg.

00:08:02.082 --> 00:08:04.496
These people attract a lot of people
working at the mine.

00:08:04.496 --> 00:08:06.246
-What's the average wage right now?

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:07.660
What would you say?

00:08:08.517 --> 00:08:11.071
Average wage?
I don't know, let's say about 80?

00:08:11.071 --> 00:08:12.795
-$80,000?
-75, 80.

00:08:12.795 --> 00:08:15.372
-Okay. And then rent as we…
-Without overtime.

00:08:15.664 --> 00:08:16.931
Without overtime.

00:08:16.931 --> 00:08:20.337
Okay, so if you're working overtime,
you can get over $100,000 here?

00:08:20.337 --> 00:08:22.107
-Easy, huh?
-Easy.

00:08:22.107 --> 00:08:24.107
And then your expenses are like nothing?

00:08:24.612 --> 00:08:27.309
Housing is like $600, I think.

00:08:28.118 --> 00:08:33.629
I found last night on their website,
$500 for a four bed, two bath house.

00:08:33.922 --> 00:08:34.740
Oh, really?

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.270
And if you work for the company,

00:08:37.195 --> 00:08:41.058
and you don't live in housing,
you get a $700 a month incentive.

00:08:42.308 --> 00:08:46.015
They'll give you $700 a month
if you're renting somewhere else.

00:08:46.023 --> 00:08:48.023
So all your… I mean, this is super cheap.

00:08:48.497 --> 00:08:50.899
I think I pay $35.

00:08:52.286 --> 00:08:53.286
-For a family?

00:08:53.907 --> 00:08:56.948
Free daycare. For the gym, two hours here.

00:08:56.948 --> 00:08:57.948
-Oh, that's cool.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.810
[Peter] Big man, what's going on?

00:09:04.495 --> 00:09:05.393
That's you, big man.

00:09:05.393 --> 00:09:08.759
Oh, um, nut'n really.
You know, just a working' town.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.956
Workin' town?
You're gonna get your working' on, right?

00:09:11.957 --> 00:09:12.957
As in workout?

00:09:13.932 --> 00:09:15.632
-Pump some iron?
-Yes, sir. Try to.

00:09:16.657 --> 00:09:18.520
-You guys say sir and ma'am out here, huh?

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.668
Yes, sir.

00:09:19.668 --> 00:09:22.197
-How's the school?
Straight A's across the board here?

00:09:22.197 --> 00:09:23.716
-Trying.
-A's and B's.

00:09:23.723 --> 00:09:24.745
A's and B's, yeah.

00:09:24.745 --> 00:09:26.430
-No C's?
-No C's.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:27.986
-You telling the truth?
-Yes, sir.

00:09:27.986 --> 00:09:29.173
One C, one C.

00:09:29.179 --> 00:09:30.668
One C. I like it. Okay.

00:09:30.669 --> 00:09:32.900
That's all right.
You'll do well in life no matter what.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:35.037
If you're fired up and you work hard

00:09:35.452 --> 00:09:36.702
and you love what you do.

00:09:37.067 --> 00:09:38.261
-See ya.
-You too.

00:09:42.279 --> 00:09:46.302
Okay, so the company
that runs this is Freeport-McMoRan

00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:51.172
And in Arizona,
copper is a massive part of the economy.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.327
From what I gathered online,

00:09:54.128 --> 00:09:56.047
the best thing I could come up with,

00:09:56.047 --> 00:10:00.878
roughly 50,000 jobs
are tied in to the copper industry.

00:10:01.252 --> 00:10:04.153
And so these kids
down at the parking lot there said

00:10:04.153 --> 00:10:07.156
we can just sort of drive
right through the mine,

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:11.568
which is awesome because it's really hard
to get this access sometimes.

00:10:12.712 --> 00:10:15.007
I think it's all about do your work,

00:10:15.007 --> 00:10:16.128
make your good money,

00:10:16.526 --> 00:10:19.670
have your stability,
raise your kids, low expenses.

00:10:20.053 --> 00:10:21.547
So they're just pulling,

00:10:22.162 --> 00:10:25.094
from my understanding,
this is the rock out of the mountain.

00:10:25.094 --> 00:10:27.294
Then somehow they get
the copper out of that.

00:10:27.882 --> 00:10:29.902
So very rough estimates.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:31.845
This mine produces

00:10:32.389 --> 00:10:36.340
a billion pounds annually of copper.

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:37.809
There we go. Tunnel time.

00:10:38.955 --> 00:10:40.005
Through the mountain.

00:10:40.006 --> 00:10:41.006
[Western music]

00:10:57.428 --> 00:10:59.125
This just keeps going and going.

00:10:59.125 --> 00:11:01.249
Oh wow. Look at this.

00:11:01.789 --> 00:11:03.482
Oh my God.

00:11:04.652 --> 00:11:08.905
So it's one of the largest
open pit mines in the world.

00:11:09.569 --> 00:11:12.088
We've been driving for a while
up and down.

00:11:13.795 --> 00:11:18.153
You can see how the road has a fence line
the whole way with barbed wire.

00:11:18.153 --> 00:11:21.198
So there's nowhere to really
pull over and get a shot

00:11:21.199 --> 00:11:22.752
except this one area.

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:28.103
[machinery whirring]

00:11:31.436 --> 00:11:33.006
-[Peter] So you work here.
-[man] I do.

00:11:33.006 --> 00:11:35.468
-So that comes out of the mountain, right?
-Yes.

00:11:35.468 --> 00:11:37.121
And then there's copper in there?

00:11:37.121 --> 00:11:38.494
-There is copper.

00:11:38.494 --> 00:11:40.440
-And what do they do? Break it down?

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.873
It depends.
Like sometimes it goes to the crushers

00:11:42.873 --> 00:11:46.073
and that goes through the whole process
to make copper out of it.

00:11:46.667 --> 00:11:48.609
And sometimes they put it in these dumps

00:11:48.622 --> 00:11:52.885
and they leach it and then
they collect those in those ponds.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:54.894
-Oh, those ponds down there?
-Yes.

00:11:55.313 --> 00:11:56.966
-So they leach it?
-Yes.

00:11:56.966 --> 00:11:59.177
-Put a chemical on it or something?
-Yes.

00:11:59.177 --> 00:12:02.777
It's some sort of chemical that basically
gets the copper out of the rock

00:12:03.169 --> 00:12:07.274
and then they send it to downstream
processing to make copper out of it.

00:12:07.274 --> 00:12:09.274
-Do you know where all this copper goes?

00:12:10.049 --> 00:12:13.249
-They have different facilities.
It depends on what the process is.

00:12:13.249 --> 00:12:16.497
But eventually, it either
comes back as copper cathode,

00:12:16.498 --> 00:12:17.948
which is those copper plates,

00:12:18.428 --> 00:12:21.841
or it's hauled to, I believe, Miami,
and that's where they…

00:12:21.841 --> 00:12:23.480
-Miami, Arizona?
-Yes.

00:12:23.919 --> 00:12:25.530
Not Florida, yes.

00:12:26.064 --> 00:12:27.541
-Okay, then it gets shipped out.

00:12:27.541 --> 00:12:29.594
-And then it gets shipped out, yes.
-Okay.

00:12:29.594 --> 00:12:31.165
I work right across…

00:12:31.716 --> 00:12:34.184
Like, if you look from the other side,
there's a small office.

00:12:34.184 --> 00:12:36.419
That's the short-range planning office.

00:12:36.419 --> 00:12:38.180
-Okay.
-So I work from there, yes.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:40.641
Okay, are these dump trucks
automated now?

00:12:40.641 --> 00:12:43.137
No, they're not. They're still manned.

00:12:43.137 --> 00:12:45.249
They're going to automate soon,
though, I think.

00:12:45.249 --> 00:12:48.034
At some point, I believe
that's where the industry is going.

00:12:48.034 --> 00:12:49.444
-How long you been out here?

00:12:49.444 --> 00:12:50.961
With the company, four years.

00:12:50.961 --> 00:12:51.961
Where are you from?

00:12:51.961 --> 00:12:53.340
I'm originally from Brazil.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:55.190
The town kind of revolves around mine.

00:12:55.682 --> 00:12:57.398
And also the schedules that people work.

00:12:57.398 --> 00:13:00.498
So it's definitely more slow-paced.

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:03.484
So how is it?
What's it like down in that world?

00:13:03.484 --> 00:13:04.423
Ah, it's a…

00:13:04.774 --> 00:13:06.948
Let's just say that
you feel very small, right?

00:13:06.948 --> 00:13:08.048
-You feel small, yeah.

00:13:08.637 --> 00:13:09.887
It's a massive operation.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:14.227
It is the largest
open copper mine in North America.

00:13:14.227 --> 00:13:16.695
The highway has moved multiple times.

00:13:16.695 --> 00:13:19.046
-Oh, they moved the highway?
-Multiple times.

00:13:19.046 --> 00:13:20.039
Gotcha.

00:13:20.039 --> 00:13:23.119
Don't quote me on that,
but it's called the Million Dollar Highway.

00:13:23.119 --> 00:13:24.380
Because it is so expensive.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:27.342
-Okay, I bet way more than a million
these days, right? That makes sense.

00:13:27.342 --> 00:13:30.069
-So they're constantly…
-Any time the mine progresses,

00:13:30.069 --> 00:13:32.422
then they have to shift the highway around.

00:13:32.948 --> 00:13:36.844
-And ecologically, I mean,
it's tough to say what's going on exactly,

00:13:36.844 --> 00:13:38.756
but better than old times, I'm sure.

00:13:38.999 --> 00:13:43.856
Yeah, I know that the company
is certified in different, like, standards.

00:13:44.084 --> 00:13:46.943
Like, I guess they have
the copper mark who just says that

00:13:46.943 --> 00:13:48.790
it is a sustainable operation and all that.

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:49.790
-Okay.

00:13:51.091 --> 00:13:53.891
All right, guys.
A lot of information here from Freeport.

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:56.200
We got a Caterpillar tire over here.

00:13:56.593 --> 00:13:58.700
So you can see the size, which is…

00:13:59.287 --> 00:14:04.411
Well, I'm coming up
a little over halfway on this thing.

00:14:05.117 --> 00:14:06.977
So that's what's moving down below there.

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:12.557
Tire weighs 8,625 pounds.

00:14:13.383 --> 00:14:16.190
And there is environmental impact for sure.

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:19.353
I'm sure some of you are concerned
about that and I totally get that.

00:14:19.353 --> 00:14:23.487
I can't speak as an expert
on that front with these things.

00:14:23.487 --> 00:14:27.265
I can say that compared to many years ago,

00:14:27.761 --> 00:14:30.358
mining operations,
have gotten much cleaner.

00:14:30.358 --> 00:14:31.958
There's also a trade-off, right?

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:33.870
If you want electricity,

00:14:34.771 --> 00:14:35.778
you need copper.

00:14:36.209 --> 00:14:37.740
If you want an electric car,

00:14:38.133 --> 00:14:39.920
you need a lot of copper.

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.270
There's no way around that.

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:43.370
Funny story, last night I was in

00:14:44.045 --> 00:14:46.507
Silver City, New Mexico,
talking to this guy.

00:14:46.507 --> 00:14:47.507
Nice older guy.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.100
And I told him I was going to Morenci.

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:52.644
And he's like, yeah, copper's…

00:14:52.644 --> 00:14:53.650
I don't like copper.

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:55.721
I'm like, what do you mean
you don't like copper?

00:14:55.721 --> 00:14:59.453
Well, back in the 1930s,
they segregated everyone.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:03.890
You know, the Latinos and the Whites,
they segregated them.

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.789
And I said, yeah, that's not good.

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:09.688
Thank God we're not in the 1930s anymore.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:11.560
That's no longer a thing.

00:15:12.025 --> 00:15:15.050
I went into a mining town recently,
which was Bagdad.

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:16.409
A few videos back, guys.

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:18.070
Went in with an open mind,

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:21.750
just wanting to see what
life was like there, how people liked it.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:24.130
And from my experience, it was positive.

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:26.390
Most people enjoyed living out there.

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:27.536
That's what they told me.

00:15:27.536 --> 00:15:28.736
Sure, some people don't,

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:31.535
but that was the information I came across.

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:35.361
And if it was the opposite,
as in people didn't like living out there,

00:15:35.361 --> 00:15:37.161
I would have kept that in the video.

00:15:37.793 --> 00:15:41.078
I just wanted to learn what it was like
to live in a company town,

00:15:41.078 --> 00:15:42.378
a modern day company town.

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:45.690
And I said, yeah,
it was actually most people liked it.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:46.893
He wasn't having it.

00:15:46.893 --> 00:15:48.959
He didn't like that copper existed.

00:15:49.238 --> 00:15:52.549
And we talked for a while longer
and on other things.

00:15:52.549 --> 00:15:57.337
And he walked across the street
and got in his Toyota Prius,

00:15:58.450 --> 00:15:59.649
and sped off.

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:02.169
I found that sort of interesting.

00:16:02.169 --> 00:16:03.808
I think a lot of people are

00:16:04.175 --> 00:16:07.222
disconnected from the things
they use on a regular basis.

00:16:07.845 --> 00:16:09.562
And copper is something

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.292
we all use every single day.

00:16:12.812 --> 00:16:14.362
And here's where it comes from.

00:16:15.013 --> 00:16:18.801
And that Brazilian guy up there
and these people down here

00:16:19.828 --> 00:16:21.506
are the people making it happen.

00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:23.220
So we can have

00:16:23.933 --> 00:16:24.933
our modern

00:16:25.664 --> 00:16:27.999
lifestyles that we all enjoy.

00:16:28.988 --> 00:16:29.988
[dump trucks whirring]

00:16:32.996 --> 00:16:35.773
See that little dump truck up there?
That's how big it is.

00:16:37.009 --> 00:16:38.309
You can hear the blasting.

00:16:38.709 --> 00:16:40.864
You just gotta follow those roads down.

00:16:41.144 --> 00:16:43.019
See if you can with your eyes there.

00:16:43.019 --> 00:16:44.632
See it just winds down the mountain.

00:16:46.776 --> 00:16:47.905
It's so wild.

00:16:47.905 --> 00:16:51.279
Over a hundred years,
one dump truck load at a time,

00:16:51.533 --> 00:16:54.487
They've gotten much bigger
since the old days.

00:16:54.487 --> 00:16:58.961
One dump truck load at a time
removed this whole mountain

00:16:59.241 --> 00:17:02.552
all the way back there
and stuff we can't even see.

00:17:03.411 --> 00:17:06.140
Camera's not even doing it justice.
I know that.

00:17:06.584 --> 00:17:08.158
But you can get a sliver here.

00:17:08.158 --> 00:17:10.625
I strongly recommend
traveling over here, you guys.

00:17:11.199 --> 00:17:12.449
If you're on a road trip,

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:14.106
this is interesting.

00:17:14.972 --> 00:17:16.840
It's 360. That's the thing.

00:17:17.738 --> 00:17:19.655
The camera's not showing 360.

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:23.488
But it's all around us right now.

00:17:24.092 --> 00:17:27.160
So there's that town we came through

00:17:27.946 --> 00:17:29.619
right next to Morenci.

00:17:30.024 --> 00:17:31.274
Just a couple miles away

00:17:31.775 --> 00:17:33.125
that looked very interesting.

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:35.380
Let's go check that place out.

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:37.274
Try to talk to some

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:39.186
locals down there.

00:17:39.727 --> 00:17:43.007
Then get a little more
of a tour of Morenci.

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:44.660
[Western music]

00:18:06.427 --> 00:18:07.825
[Peter] How you doing?

00:18:07.825 --> 00:18:09.594
-[Peter] This is beautiful.
-[woman] Yeah.

00:18:09.594 --> 00:18:11.865
-You ladies from here?
-Yeah, I was born and raised here.

00:18:11.865 --> 00:18:13.960
-No way. Can I ask you some questions?
-Yeah.

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:16.180
-Okay,
what was that like growing up here?

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:17.940
-Awesome.
-It was pretty cool.

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:19.016
It's a mining town so…

00:18:19.017 --> 00:18:22.206
You didn't have to worry about
all the strangers that are here now.

00:18:22.206 --> 00:18:24.706
-There's a lot of new people.
-Let kids run around.

00:18:25.479 --> 00:18:27.126
-Okay, so it's changed a little.

00:18:27.126 --> 00:18:28.927
-Yeah.
-Quite a bit. Not a little.

00:18:28.927 --> 00:18:31.062
Yeah, 'cause there's so many
people all over the world

00:18:31.062 --> 00:18:32.520
that have came to work here now.

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.980
Okay,
so there's people coming and going.

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:36.860
-Yep. Constantly.
-Gotcha.

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:41.117
So back in the day, people lived
in the town, mined in the town, never left?

00:18:41.117 --> 00:18:41.713
Yes.

00:18:41.713 --> 00:18:43.774
-I was just in Baghdad, Arizona.

00:18:44.318 --> 00:18:46.439
It's another Freeport town.
Oh, that's far from here.

00:18:46.439 --> 00:18:48.706
-It's far.
-It's like nine hours, isn't it?

00:18:48.706 --> 00:18:49.945
-Probably.
-Yeah.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:54.584
It's far, but the locals there
were telling me it's like that now.

00:18:54.584 --> 00:18:57.295
You let your kids go out,
it's totally safe and cool.

00:18:57.295 --> 00:18:58.986
But you're saying it's not like that here?

00:18:58.986 --> 00:19:00.278
Yeah, it's like that here.

00:19:00.278 --> 00:19:02.261
Not really. I don't think so.

00:19:02.261 --> 00:19:03.374
-No? Okay.

00:19:03.382 --> 00:19:05.542
There's too many people coming and going.

00:19:05.543 --> 00:19:07.288
There's not enough housing.

00:19:07.288 --> 00:19:10.428
-So you've got people sleeping at Circle K.
-There's a lot of drugs here.

00:19:10.428 --> 00:19:11.501
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:19:12.721 --> 00:19:14.674
Is Morenci the same, or that's here?

00:19:15.187 --> 00:19:18.180
Mostly just here in Clifton,
it's more drugs here.

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:20.024
-This is not a company town?

00:19:20.024 --> 00:19:21.624
-No, never has been.
-Okay. Okay.

00:19:22.128 --> 00:19:24.021
-Never has been? Oh.
-No.

00:19:24.021 --> 00:19:27.483
It's a historical marker or site.

00:19:27.996 --> 00:19:31.185
They won't ever be able to shut it down
like they wanted to.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:35.173
This used to be,
like back in the day, where they did mine.

00:19:35.173 --> 00:19:38.475
There actually used to be the mine
right here in the middle of the town.

00:19:38.475 --> 00:19:40.580
-The mine was right here?
-Yeah, in the middle.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:42.050
-See where this…
-It was historical.

00:19:42.050 --> 00:19:44.748
People make a lot of money
working in Morenci for the mine.

00:19:44.748 --> 00:19:45.840
Yeah, that's all they do.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:48.519
I mean, I work at a restaurant,
but I don't make that much money.

00:19:48.519 --> 00:19:50.291
But I should go up there to the mine work.

00:19:50.291 --> 00:19:51.411
They're making a lot, huh?

00:19:51.411 --> 00:19:53.011
There's more to life than money.

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:54.890
-Their rent is cheap up there too.

00:19:55.291 --> 00:20:00.130
But see, if you lose your job or whatever,
you gotta find your own place.

00:20:00.130 --> 00:20:02.731
'Cause if you lose your job
and you have a company home,

00:20:02.731 --> 00:20:04.716
then you're kind of sh*t out of luck.

00:20:04.716 --> 00:20:05.742
-You're out of town.

00:20:05.742 --> 00:20:08.520
Most of the idiots will sit,
just live in those houses.

00:20:08.521 --> 00:20:12.290
And then if they retire,
they don't have nowhere to live.

00:20:12.290 --> 00:20:14.988
-Right, so once they retire,
they have to leave town.

00:20:14.988 --> 00:20:15.950
Mm-hmm.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:19.236
So my mom, she used to work
for the company up there in Morenci.

00:20:19.673 --> 00:20:22.215
And she ended up getting breast cancer.

00:20:22.701 --> 00:20:25.612
And she also messed up her back at work.

00:20:25.915 --> 00:20:27.969
But she didn't report it right away,

00:20:27.969 --> 00:20:31.193
so she fought for a lot of years
to try to get her disability.

00:20:31.193 --> 00:20:33.969
And she wasn't able to get it
until she kept fighting for it.

00:20:33.969 --> 00:20:37.113
But they made us move out of
the company housing and everything.

00:20:37.113 --> 00:20:39.813
And we lived there for like 13 years
when we were kids.

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:47.089
[Peter] Okay, interesting.
So these towns, right next to one another,

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:49.775
have a totally different dynamic.

00:20:50.365 --> 00:20:51.865
And what I learned in Baghdad,

00:20:52.186 --> 00:20:55.285
there's randomized drug testing
with the mine.

00:20:55.285 --> 00:20:59.030
So there's not much drug usage
with the adults.

00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:03.267
And look at this.
I mean, what is that, 1913?

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:05.881
Not even that long ago
when you think about it, guys.

00:21:05.881 --> 00:21:09.829
I mean, just one
extremely long lifetime ago,

00:21:10.143 --> 00:21:11.549
this town was thriving.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:14.100
I'm sure there were bar fights,
gun fights,

00:21:14.756 --> 00:21:15.979
everything going on here.

00:21:15.979 --> 00:21:18.732
We have the Morenci Miners Union.

00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:21.605
United Steelworkers of America.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.702
Clifton Social Club over here.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:27.321
And some for sale buildings.

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:30.682
Not much of an economy here now, obviously.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:33.397
And that's how it goes. The mines run out.

00:21:33.905 --> 00:21:35.137
The people leave.

00:21:35.563 --> 00:21:37.885
The town goes downhill.

00:21:38.565 --> 00:21:40.320
Now because the mine's functioning up there

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:44.321
I think a lot of the miners
that work in Morenci live here.

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:45.870
I think that's the story.

00:21:46.371 --> 00:21:47.931
Look at these classics.

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:52.592
Just a step back in time, you guys.

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:56.151
Okay, here we go, museum.

