-Mm-hmm.

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:15.520
So, when they keep expanding,
they just move the town?

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:16.940
-Mm-hmm.
-Oh, wow.

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:19.186
They build new houses for the workers.

00:24:19.192 --> 00:24:21.300
I'll show you.
There's some pictures. Let me…

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:22.300
-Okay.

00:24:22.777 --> 00:24:24.773
And so, what was it like growing up here?

00:24:24.773 --> 00:24:27.273
Oh, I loved it. I didn't grow up here.
Let me show you.

00:24:27.274 --> 00:24:31.058
'Cause the only way you
can explain this right here,

00:24:31.058 --> 00:24:34.173
this town, the way it is here kinda,
is where I grew up.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:36.887
These started in 1882.

00:24:36.887 --> 00:24:37.887
-Mm-hmm.
-Okay.

00:24:37.887 --> 00:24:42.764
This picture was taken
in 1982 or right after.

00:24:42.764 --> 00:24:43.831
-Right around there? Okay.

00:24:43.831 --> 00:24:46.590
The last building left
was the high school right here.

00:24:46.590 --> 00:24:48.940
There actually was
four mining companies here.

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:51.981
-Okay. That was the town.
All these old buildings.

00:24:51.981 --> 00:24:54.008
Yeah. See the houses down in here?

00:24:54.008 --> 00:24:55.299
-Yeah.
-They were here.

00:24:55.300 --> 00:24:56.852
There was towns everywhere.

00:24:56.853 --> 00:25:00.745
They had a smelter up there.
They had, I think, four narrow-gauge

00:25:01.617 --> 00:25:04.474
railroads going up and down
from Clifton to here and everywhere.

00:25:04.474 --> 00:25:07.314
But see how this is,
see the houses are gone here.

00:25:07.314 --> 00:25:08.314
Yeah.

00:25:08.516 --> 00:25:12.107
Now, the people in Morenci,
Old Morenci, original,

00:25:12.539 --> 00:25:13.967
they owned their home,

00:25:14.386 --> 00:25:16.533
but they did not own the ground under it.

00:25:17.175 --> 00:25:18.294
The companies did.

00:25:19.145 --> 00:25:23.151
So, the companies
paid them for their homes and gave them…

00:25:23.151 --> 00:25:25.217
-'Cause most of them worked for the mine.
-Okay.

00:25:25.217 --> 00:25:26.885
And they gave them homes

00:25:27.645 --> 00:25:29.631
in Stargo and Plantsite.

00:25:30.395 --> 00:25:33.833
And part of Old Morenci
that weren't… like all these back here.

00:25:33.833 --> 00:25:36.199
They moved them elsewhere
and these were left.

00:25:36.424 --> 00:25:37.424
-Okay.

00:25:37.424 --> 00:25:39.970
And then they moved them into
the homes that they built.

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:42.686
And then when they rebuilt the New Morenci,

00:25:42.686 --> 00:25:45.160
the mine owns all the homes,
the company owns the homes.

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:46.234
-Up there.
-Yeah.

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:47.733
Now, not down here.

00:25:47.733 --> 00:25:50.519
-Clifton, does the company
own any of the homes here?

00:25:50.519 --> 00:25:52.369
They own part of the ground down here.

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:54.478
-That's 2010?
-Yeah.

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:57.183
So, I drove all the way up
through this thing.

00:25:57.662 --> 00:26:00.304
Did you go up to the open pit look out?

00:26:00.312 --> 00:26:01.312
Yeah, I did.

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:05.536
Okay. This is Morenci right here,
and then this is Clifton down here?

00:26:05.536 --> 00:26:06.219
-Yeah.

00:26:06.219 --> 00:26:08.179
The two towns are right next to each other.

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:11.672
Just a bit of a drop elevation-wise.

00:26:12.133 --> 00:26:13.843
This is what used to be up there.

00:26:13.843 --> 00:26:15.920
-Beautiful old buildings, huh?
-Yeah.

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:18.380
-State of the art.
-State of the art.

00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:20.680
West of St. Louis, they said.

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:22.991
-Oh, look at this.
-Now, this is when it's a little newer.

00:26:22.991 --> 00:26:25.080
See, you went through that tunnel.

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:25.890
-Yeah, I did.

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:27.423
-This is before the tunnel.

00:26:27.926 --> 00:26:29.416
This is an underground mine.

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:31.490
Can you imagine doing that by hand?

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:33.993
-So it was just like bucket by bucket?
-Yeah.

00:26:34.792 --> 00:26:36.592
Now this is Clifton, the way it was.

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:40.300
-Your main street, I just walked down here.
-This is the main street.

00:26:41.607 --> 00:26:44.397
Holler, or come and join,
one of the two.

00:26:44.397 --> 00:26:45.397
[chuckles]

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:49.171
This is the same railroad bridge
you're using now.

00:26:49.171 --> 00:26:49.899
-Okay.

00:26:49.899 --> 00:26:52.565
-The highway's over on this side.
They've changed that bridge.

00:26:52.565 --> 00:26:55.820
-So back in the day,
the air must have been really bad.

00:26:56.465 --> 00:26:58.120
When you were a kid,
do you remember that?

00:26:58.120 --> 00:27:01.330
When I was a kid growing up
in Old Morenci and Stargo,

00:27:02.155 --> 00:27:05.255
they had these big windows
and they'd open the top window.

00:27:05.255 --> 00:27:06.922
-Okay.
-That would be cool enough for us.

00:27:06.922 --> 00:27:11.079
But the sulfur smoke would come
every morning, usually around 11:00.

00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:13.030
The sulfur smoke?

00:27:13.030 --> 00:27:15.293
-From the smelter that used to be there.
-Oh.

00:27:15.627 --> 00:27:16.927
Was that really bad?

00:27:17.613 --> 00:27:18.900
-The air quality?
-I don't know.

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:19.900
You were used to it.

00:27:21.215 --> 00:27:22.215
How is it now?

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:24.095
You don't have a smelter anymore.

00:27:24.095 --> 00:27:25.268
-Okay.

00:27:25.268 --> 00:27:26.858
They took the smelter down.

00:27:26.858 --> 00:27:28.888
-[Peter] What are these?
-[woman] Are you from here.

00:27:28.888 --> 00:27:31.620
-[man] Phoenix now but I'm from here.
-[woman] You're from Phoenix?

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:33.679
I live there now, but I'm from Morenci.

00:27:33.941 --> 00:27:36.172
-[Peter] Oh, cool.
-[woman] When did you leave?

00:27:36.172 --> 00:27:38.440
I went to the service in '90

00:27:38.681 --> 00:27:42.237
and then I got out, came back,
and then… pretty small town.

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:46.134
I took off to Phoenix
to find some more opportunities.

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:49.038
This is where Circle K is now.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:52.306
-Oh, that's a little different.
-[laughs]

00:27:52.306 --> 00:27:54.901
[man] They had a terminus
for the train right here.

00:27:54.901 --> 00:27:57.610
[woman] A turnaround.
It was over more towards the store.

00:27:57.610 --> 00:28:00.875
So, sir, you grew up here.
Then you moved out. Now you've come back.

00:28:00.875 --> 00:28:03.125
What does it feel like now
when you come back?

00:28:03.540 --> 00:28:05.797
It's an incredible amount of change.
I wish…

00:28:06.177 --> 00:28:10.361
You know, I can see that it's encouraging
to see some revitalization of Chase Creek.

00:28:10.361 --> 00:28:13.120
You know, they're trying,
but there's still a way to go.

00:28:13.120 --> 00:28:17.796
I'd like to be able to see folks be able to
get involved, like with Bisbee and Jerome,

00:28:18.150 --> 00:28:20.304
and try to really revitalize it.

00:28:20.304 --> 00:28:22.663
I told a man from Tombstone one day

00:28:22.664 --> 00:28:24.875
we had a chamber mixer here one time.

00:28:24.875 --> 00:28:26.420
I told him. I said, you know what?

00:28:26.421 --> 00:28:31.125
The only thing Tombstone and Bisbee
has over Greenlee County,

00:28:31.736 --> 00:28:34.249
Clifton, and Morenci is the freeway.

00:28:35.022 --> 00:28:39.520
People go down I-10, and they see
Tombstone, Bisbee, and off they go.

00:28:40.338 --> 00:28:42.469
-So you guys want tourists here?

00:28:42.469 --> 00:28:43.441
Yes!

00:28:43.441 --> 00:28:44.290
-Okay.

00:28:44.290 --> 00:28:46.640
-Well, here we go. I'm making a video.
-[laughs]

00:28:47.371 --> 00:28:48.371
I'll get them here.

00:28:48.371 --> 00:28:50.935
And they've gotten a few new
bed and breakfast and stuff here,

00:28:50.935 --> 00:28:52.630
and they're hurting for housing.

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:54.260
You've got a county that is…

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:57.226
used to be known for cotton,
copper, or…

00:28:57.227 --> 00:28:59.317
-Cattle.
-Cattle, cotton, and copper.

00:28:59.317 --> 00:29:00.170
Yeah.

00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:01.557
-What is this picture right here?

00:29:01.557 --> 00:29:03.211
That's the Chinese store.

00:29:03.580 --> 00:29:06.537
It used to be here on Chase Creek,
I think, or no, it was on Eastside.

00:29:06.537 --> 00:29:07.870
I can't remember.

00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:09.897
I'm not quite that old. [laughs]

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:13.204
This told the rules for the employees.

00:29:13.670 --> 00:29:16.420
So the two languages here
were English and Chinese?

00:29:16.420 --> 00:29:18.052
-And Spanish.
-And Spanish?

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:19.748
More Spanish than…

00:29:20.404 --> 00:29:21.488
-Than Chinese.

00:29:21.488 --> 00:29:23.438
And they did a lot of these rock walls.

00:29:23.438 --> 00:29:25.958
The Mexican people and the Chinese.

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:29.482
The Chinese had a lot of businesses.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:31.973
They knew how to run a business.

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:34.500
So that's how miners were paid.

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.339
They would use those books.

00:29:36.339 --> 00:29:38.819
They'd get them out at the store.

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:42.858
They'd spend all their money
with these coupon books.

00:29:42.858 --> 00:29:44.751
And then sometimes
they wouldn't get a paycheck

00:29:44.751 --> 00:29:47.150
'cause they'd take it out
of their next paycheck.

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:49.763
It'd be like, you buy this from me

00:29:49.764 --> 00:29:52.297
and you spend all the coupons in it,
as an example.

00:29:52.297 --> 00:29:53.672
-You know?
-Okay, sure.

00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:56.140
And then for two weeks,

00:29:56.141 --> 00:30:00.640
you'd buy your groceries,
'cause the PD store had everything.

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:02.965
Furniture, sheets,

00:30:03.218 --> 00:30:05.642
pillowcases, blankets,
everything you needed.

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:09.785
And you bought the coupons
and you could go shopping at the store.

00:30:09.785 --> 00:30:13.278
You didn't actually buy them, they issued
them to you and they kept track.

00:30:13.278 --> 00:30:16.528
Then by the time the two weeks is over
and the pay period is over,

00:30:17.447 --> 00:30:18.985
you spent your whole paycheck.

00:30:18.985 --> 00:30:21.365
Your wife has
or somebody has on the coupons.

00:30:22.150 --> 00:30:24.250
So you owe your soul to the company store.

00:30:25.813 --> 00:30:28.755
If you ever need more of those,
you just let me know.

00:30:28.755 --> 00:30:30.425
-Here, you can have one.
-Thank you.

00:30:30.425 --> 00:30:31.671
It's a lot of great history.

00:30:31.671 --> 00:30:35.180
"Stories of Arizona's Mines
and Early Mineral Discoveries."

00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:36.912
-[Peter] Fantastic.
-[woman] Jack did this?

00:30:36.912 --> 00:30:38.333
When did he do this?

00:30:38.333 --> 00:30:39.333
They're old.

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:42.380
[Peter] You have a virtual tour on YouTube,
correct?

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:46.095
Yeah. And the library,
the public library did that.

00:30:46.095 --> 00:30:49.846
So if people want to know more
about this place, go to that video.

00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:53.549
[man] There was a lot of stores
up and down here.

00:30:53.549 --> 00:30:54.950
There was restaurants.

00:30:54.950 --> 00:30:56.900
Too many bars, I guess, really.

00:30:56.900 --> 00:30:59.150
There's about three or four bars, you know.

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:01.509
-Is it sad for you to see it so quiet now?

00:31:02.617 --> 00:31:04.220
Or it's just the way life goes?

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:08.118
It's sad for people that left and come back
and they see it the way it is now.

00:31:09.330 --> 00:31:11.623
Because I had a schoolmate come back.

00:31:11.623 --> 00:31:15.296
We had a little girl's reunion
one time when we graduated in '61.

00:31:15.633 --> 00:31:18.517
And two of them just cried.
They said, "I won't come back."

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:21.126
Because not only was

00:31:22.057 --> 00:31:23.963
the town different, new town,

00:31:24.695 --> 00:31:25.967
their house was gone.

00:31:27.140 --> 00:31:28.790
The school was gone.

00:31:28.790 --> 00:31:30.260
The stores were gone.

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:31.953
All those pictures I showed you in there,

00:31:32.654 --> 00:31:33.654
they're gone.

00:31:33.963 --> 00:31:36.893
And it was a culture shock
coming and seeing

00:31:37.453 --> 00:31:39.148
than staying here and living with it.

00:31:39.148 --> 00:31:40.821
You know what I'm saying? It's different.

00:31:40.821 --> 00:31:42.908
-You saw it go gradually.
-Yeah.

00:31:43.575 --> 00:31:45.774
-To some degree.
-It wasn't like a, ugh, like…

00:31:48.558 --> 00:31:50.125
This is a newer business.

00:31:51.115 --> 00:31:53.521
Look at that.
The People's Bank and Trust Co.

00:31:54.575 --> 00:31:56.161
And yeah, they're trying.

00:31:57.088 --> 00:31:58.536
It's got the bones for it.

00:31:59.654 --> 00:32:01.083
-How you doing?
-Hi.

00:32:01.367 --> 00:32:02.665
When did you start this?

00:32:02.665 --> 00:32:04.791
We just opened Monday.

00:32:04.790 --> 00:32:06.103
-Are you serious?
-Yeah.

00:32:06.103 --> 00:32:07.663
-We're super into it.
-Okay.

00:32:08.089 --> 00:32:11.010
We want to revitalize Chase Creek
and get it popping again.

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:14.069
It used to be like
a main commercial district

00:32:14.069 --> 00:32:16.371
and I think it'd be fun
to get it going again.

00:32:16.371 --> 00:32:18.218
So, one building at a time.

00:32:18.218 --> 00:32:21.095
So, we bought this building
and gutted it and renovated it.

00:32:21.095 --> 00:32:23.595
And upstairs, we have an Airbnb.

00:32:24.002 --> 00:32:27.162
You got food products. You got shirts.
You got a little bit of everything.

00:32:27.162 --> 00:32:30.245
Yep. A lot of it's local artisans
just supporting their crafts.

00:32:30.245 --> 00:32:32.353
We have surprisingly talented people here.

00:32:32.985 --> 00:32:35.290
-Are you from here?
-No, I'm from New Mexico.

00:32:35.290 --> 00:32:39.915
And then I went up to Utah for college
and married a guy, whatever.

00:32:39.915 --> 00:32:41.102
But we've been back…

00:32:41.102 --> 00:32:43.710
We've been here for like eight years.

00:32:43.710 --> 00:32:44.743
-How is it?

00:32:44.744 --> 00:32:46.088
-I love it.
-You love it?

00:32:46.088 --> 00:32:49.621
I didn't think I would. I was like,
I was dreading the small town stuff.

00:32:49.621 --> 00:32:51.405
Thinking, oh my gosh, what are we doing?

00:32:51.405 --> 00:32:52.828
But my sister had lived here

00:32:52.829 --> 00:32:55.231
and we came and visited
the year before we moved actually

00:32:55.231 --> 00:32:57.591
and thought
what a cute little charming town.

00:32:58.105 --> 00:33:01.335
And then we ended up moving here,
which we didn't expect.

00:33:01.795 --> 00:33:02.795
-That's so cool.

00:33:03.046 --> 00:33:06.461
Do you notice a big difference
between here, Clifton, and Morenci?

00:33:07.153 --> 00:33:10.158
Um, Morenci gets maintained more

00:33:10.159 --> 00:33:11.551
'cause the mine has more money.

00:33:12.307 --> 00:33:15.379
-Okay, so what do you think
of the company town concept?

00:33:17.738 --> 00:33:20.424
I think it could be like
a corrupt sort of thing,

00:33:20.424 --> 00:33:22.348
but I think it's actually run pretty well.

00:33:22.348 --> 00:33:23.651
-Morenci?
-Mm-hmm.

00:33:23.651 --> 00:33:24.841
-Yeah.
-I think it's charming.

00:33:24.841 --> 00:33:27.360
We live in a Morenci house
in Clifton actually,

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:29.600
but they have like
one little section in Clifton

00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:31.832
that's technically Morenci, but not.

00:33:32.115 --> 00:33:34.320
-Okay, so the mine
owns the house that you live in.

00:33:34.321 --> 00:33:35.061
Mm-hmm.

00:33:35.061 --> 00:33:36.342
-You pay rent.
-Mm-hmm.

00:33:36.775 --> 00:33:37.775
But cheap rent.

00:33:37.775 --> 00:33:39.540
-It's very cheap, right?
-Mm-hmm.

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:43.526
Yeah, we were kind of nervous
'cause we wanted to buy

00:33:43.527 --> 00:33:46.293
a beautiful big fancy house
and there just aren't here.

00:33:46.607 --> 00:33:47.182
-Yeah.

00:33:47.182 --> 00:33:51.349
But it's kind of nice 'cause then everyone
just sort of is on equal footing that way.

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:54.584
You feel like a camaraderie
sort of that way. I don't know.

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:57.926
Like we're in identical houses,
but you still have your individuality,

00:33:57.926 --> 00:34:00.211
but then you're not
competing with the Joneses sort of

00:34:00.212 --> 00:34:01.162
'cause just we all…

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:03.842
you can go on more vacations with your kids

00:34:03.842 --> 00:34:05.681
because you're not paying it all in rent.

00:34:06.514 --> 00:34:08.438
But I don't, I don't feel that competition,

00:34:08.438 --> 00:34:10.931
especially after Utah,
which you do feel, you know?

00:34:14.893 --> 00:34:16.698
Far outpost in the desert.

00:34:17.278 --> 00:34:18.786
Old time feeling.

00:34:18.786 --> 00:34:20.792
Slow pace of life.

00:34:21.493 --> 00:34:23.027
Clifton Hotel and Bar.

00:34:23.986 --> 00:34:26.466
The assessor's office is looking spiffy.

00:34:26.892 --> 00:34:28.342
It's the fire department too.

00:34:28.970 --> 00:34:30.154
That is beautiful.

00:34:30.854 --> 00:34:32.449
Right up on the hillsides.

00:34:33.102 --> 00:34:34.453
Cute little zones here.

00:34:34.453 --> 00:34:35.734
Right into the cliffs.

00:34:37.056 --> 00:34:40.551
San Francisco River flowing through town.

00:34:41.257 --> 00:34:42.384
And here you go.

00:34:43.214 --> 00:34:46.760
Some of those original mining homes.

00:34:47.380 --> 00:34:50.653
I'm sure there's some
multi-generational homes.

00:34:52.146 --> 00:34:55.020
Families that grew up here
and then their kids take it over

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:56.188
and their kids take it over.

00:34:56.188 --> 00:34:58.088
I think there's gotta be some of that.

00:34:59.166 --> 00:35:00.478
[Peter] Are you guys brothers?

00:35:00.478 --> 00:35:02.506
-No, that's my son.
-Yeah, that's my dad.

00:35:02.506 --> 00:35:03.593
-Oh, wow.

00:35:03.593 --> 00:35:05.643
Yeah, thanks.
You just made his day, dude.

00:35:05.875 --> 00:35:08.743
Okay. So the homes down here,
you guys are buying?

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:10.063
Yeah. All these are our owned.

00:35:10.063 --> 00:35:11.480
-But you're working up at the mine?

00:35:11.480 --> 00:35:12.940
-Yeah, yeah. We work up there.

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:14.390
-How things going these days?

00:35:14.707 --> 00:35:15.850
-Good so far.

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:18.107
-I was born and raised here. So was he.
-No way.

00:35:18.107 --> 00:35:20.436
I've been working for, what,
30 years now. So…

00:35:20.920 --> 00:35:22.560
And copper's good right now, right?

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:24.102
Copper's good. Yeah, I can't complain.

00:35:24.102 --> 00:35:28.997
It fluctuates. But yeah, it's been
the highest since I've been working, right?

00:35:28.997 --> 00:35:30.210
-No way.
-Oh, yeah.

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:33.557
So do you know when copper's high,
you notice a difference with employment?

00:35:33.557 --> 00:35:36.020
-There's more money floating around?
-Yeah.

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:39.440
So your life just completely revolves
around that one mineral.

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:41.170
-Pretty much, yeah.
-[laughter]

00:35:41.170 --> 00:35:42.625
Yeah, that's about it.

00:35:42.625 --> 00:35:44.096
Around here anyways, yeah.

00:35:44.096 --> 00:35:45.277
-But there's…

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:48.620
-We have byproducts too,
but that's our main…

00:35:48.970 --> 00:35:51.281
-But there's decades in that mine, right?

00:35:51.281 --> 00:35:52.175
Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:35:52.175 --> 00:35:56.130
I'm like, what, third or fourth generation
miner in my family.

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:57.130
-Oh, no way.

00:35:57.562 --> 00:35:58.680
Yeah.

00:35:58.680 --> 00:36:02.100
And then possibly my great-grandfather.
I'm not too sure. I can't remember.

00:36:02.100 --> 00:36:04.902
-Okay, so it's in the blood.
-It's, yeah.

00:36:04.902 --> 00:36:06.380
-Jacob. Nice to meet you.
-Peter.

00:36:06.381 --> 00:36:07.381
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:36:07.747 --> 00:36:10.152
-Oh, that's cool.
-My son, Jacob Jr.

00:36:10.152 --> 00:36:12.661
-Jacob Jr. Nice.
-Jacob Jr.

00:36:12.661 --> 00:36:16.691
He actually works over at the mine
in Tucson in Sarita for Freeport.

00:36:16.691 --> 00:36:19.410
-Yeah, Freeport's everywhere.
-Same company, just different area.

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:20.770
My daughter works…

00:36:20.770 --> 00:36:24.141
She's in the mine, but she actually works
for a contractor, Napa.

00:36:24.141 --> 00:36:26.828
-Okay.
-And then my other boy, he's the youngest.

00:36:27.350 --> 00:36:30.700
He's actually a mechanic in the garage,
which I'm the supervisor at.

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:34.441
Freeport sent me out of the country,
so I've been to Indonesia.

00:36:34.813 --> 00:36:36.440
-Yeah, so…
-How was that?

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:39.764
It was great. It was good down there.
It was nice. I liked it.

00:36:39.764 --> 00:36:42.993
We all had an opportunity
to move down there, work there, but…

00:36:43.370 --> 00:36:45.344
Yeah, my wife works for Freeport as well,

00:36:45.345 --> 00:36:47.409
So we all… We're all working there.

00:36:47.992 --> 00:36:50.995
-And then everyone knows everyone?
-Yeah, everybody here.

00:36:50.995 --> 00:36:52.165
-So you don't worry about…

00:36:52.166 --> 00:36:54.865
You're either family
or close friends, right?

00:36:55.331 --> 00:36:59.252
Okay, so it's very hot here in the summer
obviously but you just rip up the mountain.

00:36:59.252 --> 00:37:00.928
Yeah. Two hours, two and a half hours.

00:37:00.928 --> 00:37:03.697
Two and a half hours.
You're at, like, 8,000, 9,000 feet?

00:37:03.697 --> 00:37:04.435
Yup.

00:37:04.435 --> 00:37:06.437
And then it's cool up there in the summers?

00:37:06.437 --> 00:37:10.133
It's cool up there.
I think the highest it gets, maybe 80, 85.

00:37:10.553 --> 00:37:11.977
85 is, like, really hot up there,

00:37:11.977 --> 00:37:14.737
but most of the time
it's between 75 and 80.

00:37:14.737 --> 00:37:15.673
-Cold nights?

00:37:15.673 --> 00:37:18.421
Oh, nights are like,
yeah, get down to the 40s, 50s.

00:37:18.421 --> 00:37:19.823
-Midsummer?
-Summer, yeah.

00:37:19.823 --> 00:37:21.440
That's during the midsummer, you know.

00:37:21.440 --> 00:37:24.470
Heater at nighttime, you know,
depending on how you are.

00:37:24.470 --> 00:37:26.214
-In the summer in Arizona?

00:37:26.214 --> 00:37:29.036
During the day, yeah, you turn on
the AC for a little while. [chuckles]

00:37:29.036 --> 00:37:30.920
And then you come down here
and it's baking out.

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:35.201
Yeah, and then you head down the hill
and you're like, oh, man, this is terrible.

00:37:35.828 --> 00:37:37.056
-But winters are nice here?

00:37:37.056 --> 00:37:38.756
Yeah, winters are great here, yeah.

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:41.840
You say Morenci is a mining camp?

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:44.380
Yeah, that's what it's really considered.

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:45.770
-Because they move it around?

00:37:45.770 --> 00:37:48.515
Because, yeah,
it's all owned by Freeport, the mine, yeah.

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:51.060
I talked to Tammy at the museum.
She said that.

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:53.320
Yeah, I was going to say,
if you hit them up…

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:54.514
-Tammy's great.

00:37:54.514 --> 00:37:56.417
You guys have a lot of big pickup trucks.

00:37:56.417 --> 00:37:57.691
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:37:57.691 --> 00:37:58.968
Is that where the money goes?

00:37:58.968 --> 00:37:59.968
Pretty much, yeah.

00:38:04.307 --> 00:38:06.750
Yeah, we got horses too,
so we got, you know.

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:09.329
-Horses here?
-Yeah, they're up at the corrals.

00:38:09.329 --> 00:38:11.019
-Right up the road.
-You have corrals here?

00:38:11.019 --> 00:38:12.021
Yeah, up on top, yeah.

00:38:12.021 --> 00:38:15.151
And that's actually Freeport-owned,
so we rent them from Freeport.

00:38:15.151 --> 00:38:18.539
I think we pay, like,
50 bucks a month for a nice little stable.

00:38:18.539 --> 00:38:20.589
-Get his license plate right there.
-Yeah.

00:38:23.418 --> 00:38:25.440
-[Peter] Life is good out here?
-Oh, yeah.

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:26.440
Cheap, easy.

00:38:27.713 --> 00:38:28.713
Overtime.

00:38:29.660 --> 00:38:31.685
-Overtime? Good overtime?
-Yeah.

00:38:31.987 --> 00:38:35.150
There's a lot of money that could be made
if you really want it, you know.

00:38:35.150 --> 00:38:37.453
It's hard to hire people from the outside.

00:38:37.453 --> 00:38:40.278
-Why is that?
-'Cause they come in from a bigger city,

00:38:40.278 --> 00:38:41.933
and they see this place and they're like,

00:38:41.933 --> 00:38:44.153
"Oh, what's there to do around here?"

00:38:44.600 --> 00:38:46.829
So, most of the time
they don't end up living here

00:38:46.829 --> 00:38:48.879
or they stay here
for a few months and they're gone

00:38:48.879 --> 00:38:51.573
'cause there's just not
too much to do around here, right?

00:38:51.573 --> 00:38:55.221
Besides traveling three hours
to Phoenix or Tucson.

00:38:55.221 --> 00:38:59.710
Yeah, but if you're into horses,
you're into your mountain cabin.

00:38:59.710 --> 00:39:02.123
Yeah, if you're an outdoorsman,
this is perfect right here.

00:39:02.123 --> 00:39:04.233
-Yeah.
-You know everyone in town.

00:39:04.233 --> 00:39:05.633
-You got your buddies.
-Yeah.

00:39:06.085 --> 00:39:07.085
Sounds pretty sweet.

00:39:07.085 --> 00:39:08.612
During the week you're working, right?

00:39:08.612 --> 00:39:12.747
So, there's, it's not like you're gonna
do something after work.

00:39:12.747 --> 00:39:13.833
You're pretty much tired.

00:39:13.833 --> 00:39:15.939
You want to come home,
eat, sleep, go back to work.

00:39:15.939 --> 00:39:20.571
It's the weekends where you can go
spend your money outside of this place.

00:39:20.571 --> 00:39:22.440
Yeah, and you're not spending much here?

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:24.310
Yeah, no. It's, like I said, it's…

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:25.738
-It's all about the truck.

00:39:25.738 --> 00:39:27.788
-That's where you spend it.
-Yeah. [laughs]

00:39:31.199 --> 00:39:32.199
[wind blowing]

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:40.761
Well, this is what the streets are like
here in Morenci, Saturday afternoon.

00:39:42.364 --> 00:39:44.551
We were way up there in the mine

00:39:44.984 --> 00:39:47.432
and it's just neighborhood
after neighborhood

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:49.037
looking like this.

00:39:52.581 --> 00:39:56.587
These must be the one bedroom,
one bath, or studio options,

00:39:57.168 --> 00:40:00.558
which I saw online are $200.

00:40:05.859 --> 00:40:07.725
It's just this contained life.

00:40:09.176 --> 00:40:11.176
That's what I'm feeling here in Morenci.

00:40:12.812 --> 00:40:15.097
Well look, they got
all these little parks here.

00:40:16.812 --> 00:40:18.103
Home of the Wildcats.

00:40:18.703 --> 00:40:19.703
Welcome to the hill.

00:40:22.854 --> 00:40:25.997
Okay, just got a tip from a guy
that didn't want to be on camera

00:40:25.998 --> 00:40:30.595
saying that this part of town over here
is the nice part of town.

00:40:30.595 --> 00:40:34.325
These are the managers
or the higher paid positions.

00:40:34.792 --> 00:40:36.392
Their housing's a bit different.

00:40:36.907 --> 00:40:41.229
It's almost like a separate zone
of Morenci, like an island.

00:40:41.910 --> 00:40:43.640
Beautiful scenery here, though.

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:44.640
Look at this.

00:40:45.452 --> 00:40:47.548
Lights starting to soften a little bit.

00:40:47.548 --> 00:40:49.500
Desert colors come alive.

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:52.171
And here we go.

00:40:53.198 --> 00:40:54.595
Yeah, feels different.

00:40:55.150 --> 00:40:57.730
Got these beautiful cypress trees.

00:41:01.443 --> 00:41:03.474
So this is wealthy Morenci.

00:41:05.975 --> 00:41:07.771
Not too many homes out here.

00:41:09.820 --> 00:41:11.588
A little more space between them.

00:41:11.588 --> 00:41:12.588
A little bit bigger.

00:41:13.855 --> 00:41:16.378
Nice park in the middle.

00:41:22.654 --> 00:41:23.957
And I think that's it.

00:41:24.353 --> 00:41:25.420
That's about it.

00:41:25.835 --> 00:41:27.696
The Hollywood Hills of Morenci.

00:41:28.987 --> 00:41:30.714
[children playing]

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:39.160
[man] My boy's right there.
The one looking this way.

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:40.443
-Is he pretty good?
-Oh yeah.

00:41:41.183 --> 00:41:43.017
-So people mostly dig it out here, yeah?

00:41:43.017 --> 00:41:43.873
Oh yeah.

00:41:43.873 --> 00:41:45.891
And like there's
all different departments, right?

00:41:45.891 --> 00:41:46.903
You got your dirt work.

00:41:46.903 --> 00:41:49.925
You got your SX plant.

00:41:49.925 --> 00:41:51.580
You got the mill. You got…

00:41:51.580 --> 00:41:54.389
So it's like a different range of people.

00:41:54.389 --> 00:41:56.839
Engineers.
There's people from all over the world.

00:41:57.183 --> 00:41:59.013
-What you guys got going here
is unbelievable.

00:41:59.013 --> 00:42:01.503
I work for a company called Swap
Constructions. A contractor.

00:42:01.503 --> 00:42:02.136
-Okay.

00:42:02.136 --> 00:42:03.675
And I run heavy equipment for them.

00:42:03.675 --> 00:42:04.420
-Right on.

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:06.310
-So you don't work for Freeport?
-No.

00:42:06.310 --> 00:42:07.512
I'm contracted out for them.

00:42:07.512 --> 00:42:08.810
-Contracted out for them.

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:10.576
-So there's a lot of that going on?
-Oh yeah.

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:12.780
-That's fun.
-It's fun?

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:14.200
The dirt work part's fun.

00:42:14.200 --> 00:42:16.803
-It's like being a kid and playing around?
-Oh yeah. Big sandbox.

00:42:17.643 --> 00:42:19.480
-You started at 16?
-Yeah. As a laborer.

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:20.430
What's it go up to?

00:42:21.630 --> 00:42:23.106
I'm up to 35 right now.

00:42:23.106 --> 00:42:25.006
-Nice.
-And I still have room to climb.

00:42:25.325 --> 00:42:27.376
Like we were working on
this pipe corridor

00:42:27.376 --> 00:42:30.010
and if you look over the edge
from off the high wall.

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:33.060
It's like 4,000 foot to the bottom
to the pond down below you.

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:34.699
Long ways.

00:42:36.122 --> 00:42:37.704
-So high risk?
-Yeah.

00:42:38.845 --> 00:42:40.524
Yeah. No room for human error.

00:42:41.095 --> 00:42:43.185
There's randomized drug testing, right?

00:42:43.185 --> 00:42:44.165
Oh yeah. Well yeah.

00:42:44.165 --> 00:42:46.218
I just got random a couple months ago.

00:42:46.733 --> 00:42:51.020
Okay. So no adults are really using drugs
unless they want to get kicked out of town.

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:52.020
Yeah. Exactly.

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:53.262
That's what happens?

00:42:53.262 --> 00:42:55.462
Oh yeah.
You get blackballed for seven years.

00:42:55.959 --> 00:42:56.829
Okay.

00:42:56.829 --> 00:42:59.643
And that's off of every
Freeport-McMoRan site.

00:42:59.643 --> 00:43:01.489
That's Chino, Bagdad.

00:43:01.855 --> 00:43:05.805
So if you piss dirty here, then you can't
go work at none of them places either.

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:08.468
And that even detects weed?

00:43:08.468 --> 00:43:09.238
Yep.

00:43:09.238 --> 00:43:10.588
-So no weed, nothing?
-Nope.

00:43:11.960 --> 00:43:15.206
And nobody plays with that 'cause
their whole life gets thrown upside down.

00:43:15.206 --> 00:43:18.328
It would suck if you put…
Like I got 11 years up here.

00:43:18.328 --> 00:43:20.100
Almost 11. March will be 11 years.

00:43:20.100 --> 00:43:21.100
Okay.

00:43:21.373 --> 00:43:23.220
It'd just be ruined.

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:25.601
It'd just be a waste of 11 years.

00:43:25.601 --> 00:43:28.348
You'd be kicked out of your house.
No health insurance. No job.

00:43:28.348 --> 00:43:29.553
Nothing.

00:43:29.553 --> 00:43:32.020
-Your wife would hate you.
-I'll guarantee it. [laughs]

00:43:36.251 --> 00:43:38.451
So only a couple of
restaurants open tonight.

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:40.916
Miner's Diner and Bar.

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:43.620
This is what it's gonna be.

00:43:46.840 --> 00:43:48.476
Are these guys good chefs back there?

00:43:48.476 --> 00:43:50.576
Yeah, that one's the best one right there.

00:43:51.045 --> 00:43:52.289
Best chef in Morenci?

00:43:52.290 --> 00:43:54.033
Yeah. Best chef in Morenci. [giggles]

00:43:54.385 --> 00:43:55.485
-All right. All right.

00:43:56.806 --> 00:43:58.158
Mexican options.

00:43:59.220 --> 00:44:00.754
Enchiladas you said?

00:44:00.754 --> 00:44:02.384
-Those are excellent.
-Mm-hmm.

00:44:02.384 --> 00:44:03.536
-Chicken.
-Chicken?

00:44:03.536 --> 00:44:04.536
-Yes.
-Perfect.

00:44:04.841 --> 00:44:06.641
-Can I get your name, please?
-Peter.

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:08.750
Are you from Morenci?

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:10.450
I am. I'm in high school.

00:44:11.016 --> 00:44:12.039
-How's the high school?

00:44:12.039 --> 00:44:13.515
It's good. It's really good.

00:44:14.197 --> 00:44:15.611
The school district is good.

00:44:15.612 --> 00:44:18.040
We do pretty good, academic-wise.

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:19.199
-Oh, you do well?
-Yes.

00:44:19.199 --> 00:44:20.701
Oh, that's cool. Nice.

00:44:22.158 --> 00:44:23.440
Alright, great. Thank you.

00:44:23.446 --> 00:44:26.413
Of course. Is there anything I can get you?
salsa, sour cream, or anything?

00:44:26.413 --> 00:44:28.464
-I'll take a salsa. Thank you.
-Perfect.

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:39.550
Mm-hmm.

00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:43.963
Basic.

00:44:43.963 --> 00:44:44.963
Straightforward.

00:44:49.690 --> 00:44:51.050
Morenci style.

00:44:55.610 --> 00:44:56.610
Good beans.

00:44:57.652 --> 00:44:58.652
Tender chicken.

00:45:00.034 --> 00:45:01.034
Mm-hmm.

00:45:03.591 --> 00:45:04.591
Hits the spot.

00:45:05.106 --> 00:45:06.821
For sure after today.

00:45:07.857 --> 00:45:09.245
That was a long one.

00:45:09.245 --> 00:45:11.564
Started in Silver City, New Mexico.

00:45:12.928 --> 00:45:14.178
A couple hours from here.

00:45:14.949 --> 00:45:16.591
Beautiful, beautiful countryside.

00:45:17.963 --> 00:45:21.404
This part of the country
is quite special for sure.

00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:28.038
Interesting place.

00:45:28.038 --> 00:45:29.473
Interesting way of living.

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:34.343
The company town
holds strong here in 2025.

00:45:34.343 --> 00:45:36.555
Morenci, Arizona.

00:45:36.990 --> 00:45:40.391
I'd say at least 99% of Americans

00:45:40.613 --> 00:45:43.157
wouldn't even think
a company town still existed.

00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:44.535
But it does.

00:45:44.535 --> 00:45:47.435
Also have another company town video
somewhere around here.

00:45:47.725 --> 00:45:49.725
It's called Bagdad without an H.

00:45:49.725 --> 00:45:50.873
Bagdad, Arizona.

00:45:51.239 --> 00:45:53.561
Thanks for coming along
on that journey, you guys.

00:45:53.561 --> 00:45:54.561
Until the next one.

00:45:54.561 --> 00:45:55.561
[Western music]

